==English==

===Etymology===
The original saying may have been ''keep the wolf from the gate'', which dates from at least 1470.<ref>{{cite-book|author=John Hardyng|authorlink=John Hardyng|chapter=The. xcviii. Chapiter. The Lamentacyon of the Maker of this Booke, and His Counsayle to My Lorde of Yorke, for Good Rule in the Realme of Englande|title=The Chronicle of Ihon Hardyng,{{nb...|in Metre, from the First Begynnyng of Englande, vnto the Reigne of Edwarde the Fourth, where He Made an End of His Chronicle. And from that Tyme is Added with a Continuacion of the Storie in Prose to this Our Tyme. Now First Emprinted, Gathered out of Diuerse and Soundrie Autours, of Most Certain Knowelage and Substanciall Credit, that either in Latin, or els in Our Mother Toungue, haue Writen of the Affaires of Englande.}}|location=London|publisher=In officina [[w:Richard Grafton|Richardi Graftoni]]|month=January|year=1543|oclc=702183961|2ndauthor=John Hardyng; Richard Grafton|title2=The Chronicle of Iohn Hardyng.{{nb...|Containing an Account of Public Transactions from the Earliest Period of English History to the Beginning of the Reign of King Edward the Fourth. Together with the Continuation by Richard Grafton, to the Thirty Fourth Year of King Henry the Eighth. The Former Part Collated with Two Manuscripts of the Author’s Own Time; the Last, with Grafton’s Duplicate Edition. To which are Added a Biographical and Literary Preface, and an Index, by Henry Ellis.}}|location2=London|publisher2=Printed for [[w:Rivington (publishers)|F[rancis] C[harles] and J[ohn] Rivington]]; [''et al.'']|year2=1812|section2=stanza XII|page2=181|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=75hTAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA181|oclc2=220598405|passage=Endowe hym now with noble sapience, / By whiche he maye '''the wolf werre frome the gate''', / For wisedome is more worth in all defence, / Then any gold or riches congregate;{{nb...}}.}}</ref> By the 1500s the saying had become ''keep the wolf from the door'', with the current meaning that it bears: see, for example, the 1645 quotation.

There is a suggestion that the phrase may have originated from French or German phrases. Compare the {{cog|fr|manger comme un loup||eat like a wolf}}, and the {{cog|de|Wolfsmagen|lit=wolf’s stomach}} means “a keen appetite”.<ref>{{cite-journal|journal=[[w:Notes and Queries|Notes and Queries: A Medium of Inter-communication for Literary Men, Artists, Antiquaries, Genealogists, etc.]]|series=2nd Series|location=London|publisher=[[w:George Bell & Sons|Bell and Daldy]]|date=8 August 1857|volume=4|issue=84|page=115|oclc=887525157}}.</ref>

